      "independent_solution": "Solve both entire systems in ordered pairs (x,y). I: x^2-2y^2=14 and 2x-3y=1 imply x=(1+3y)/2 and y^2+6y-55=(y-5)(y+11)=0. Thus the COMPLETE set is {(8,5),(-16,-11)}. Both pairs satisfy both equations: 64-50=14,16-15=1 and 256-242=14,-32+33=1. II: 3xy-2y^2=30 and 3x-2y=10 imply y(3x-2y)=30, hence y=3 and x=16/3. Its COMPLETE set is {(16/3,3)}, checked by 48-18=30 and 16-6=10. Substitution in I is reversible and the quadratic has exactly two roots; II forces both coordinates uniquely. These pre-disclosure solutions match the author.",
